{"question_id": "2038", "paper_id": "700", "policy": "E2", "evidence": {"text": "", "items": [], "rejected_items": [], "evidence_tokens": 0, "support_tokens": 0, "fact_tokens": 0, "num_supported_items": 0, "num_accepted_items": 0, "num_rejected_items": 0, "num_accepted_facts": 0, "num_rejected_facts": 0, "exact_span_support_rate": null, "accepted_exact_span_support_rate": null, "zero_exact_support": true, "invalid_format": true, "empty_extraction": false, "fail_closed": true, "failure_reason": "malformed_output", "extraction_valid": false, "reached_extraction_token_limit": true, "evidence_hash": "12ae32cb1ec02d01eda3581b127c1fee3b0dc53572ed6baf239721a03d82e126", "max_evidence_tokens": 128, "grounding_note": "Exact source support is mechanical, not semantic entailment. Facts are conservatively copied subspans, not free paraphrases."}, "raw_output": "[{\"source_chunk_id\": \"b490e4a32a6f10cdcfc2978b\", \"support_span\": \"The final label distribution for the data is given in Figure FIGREF17 . In initial investigations of the data we noticed that very few instances had multiple labels, e.g., “comment,complaint”. In the English training data this amounted to 4% of the data. We decided to ignore those additional labels (just picked the first in case of multiple labels) and treat the problem as a single-class classification problem. This was motivated by the fact that some labels were expected to be easily confused. Finally, there were some labels in the data that did not map to any of the labels in the task description (i.e., `undetermined', `undefined', `nonsense' and `noneless', they were presumably typos) so we mapped them all to the `meaningless' label. This frames the task as a 5-class classification problem with the following classes: bug, comment, complaint, meaningless and request.\", \"normalized_claim\": \"5-class classification problem with the following classes:", "prompt_hash": "7747f27b98432aaeae673d82de8df59684278d0bcd9e2e23299e5623dc7a0a2c", "extraction_c